@@ -232,6 +232,27 @@ Tested on one machine: Windows 11, AMD RX 6700 XT.
 > moment). To look further, use a diagnostics build with
 > `VICTORY_D3D12_WARP=1`, which renders on the software adapter.
 
+`VICTORY_RECOVER_REMOVAL=1` opts into removal preservation (default off;
+keep it off by default until a title-equipped validation run). It enables
+DRED before device creation and copies readable cloneable process RAM, saves
+and persistent storage into `<title data>/recovery/snapshot-*` before exit.
+Completed generations have a `COMPLETE` hash manifest; `.partial` directories
+are incomplete and never replace an older generation. Guarded title RAM is
+read from a clone without disarming the live guards. Each removal produces
+one JSON line in `crashes.log`, with pending breadcrumb count (`null` when
+unavailable), preserved artifacts, attempt status and outcome.
+
+This build cannot safely resume a running title: device children are cached
+for process lifetime, and lost GPU writes have no replayable safe point.
+It makes zero automatic title recovery attempts, exits with `0xE0670001`
+after verified preservation (`0xE0670002` if preservation is incomplete),
+and prints the recovery folder. These are forensic snapshots, not loadable
+save states; unsaved progress and shared-page consistency are not guaranteed.
+Do not copy partial files over live saves. The no-title WARP diagnostics
+probe releases all old objects and verifies device/library/heap recreation
+and submission three times, then refuses a fourth attempt. That probe is
+not evidence of live title resume. With the flag off, legacy handling stays.
+
 What each part does today and what it still needs:
 
 | Subsystem | Today | Needs |
